# Agent swarm -- local operator configuration.
#
#   cp .env.example .env    (or run `make bootstrap`, which does it for you)
#
# .env is gitignored and is read by every script in scripts/ through
# scripts/lib/common.sh. It is OPERATOR configuration: which project, which
# environment, where things live. It is NOT where the platform's behaviour is
# configured.
#
# Two things deliberately do not belong in this file:
#
#   * SECRETS. No API key, no token, no password. Tenant provider keys live in
#     Secret Manager and are written by `scripts/create-secrets.sh`, which never
#     takes a key as an argument (argv is world-readable through `ps`).
#   * CONCURRENCY LIMITS. They live in Firestore `pools/*` documents so an admin
#     can change them through the admin API without a redeploy. Setting them
#     here would give you a number that is true only until the next deploy.
#
# Every value below is the default that scripts/lib/common.sh already applies,
# so an empty .env works. Uncomment only what you are actually changing.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Target project
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# SHARED project. It holds a live GKE cluster (agents-staging), a VPC
# (agents-staging-vpc) and 12 service accounts owned by other teams. Nothing in
# this repo may touch them; scripts/lib/common.sh carries the deny-list.
PROJECT_ID=saga-agents-staging
REGION=us-central1
ZONE=us-central1-a

# dev | staging | prod. Selects terraform/environments/<env>/<env>.tfvars, the
# state prefix, and the image channel tag. `prod` additionally makes destroy.sh
# refuse without --allow-prod.
ENVIRONMENT=dev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data plane
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# NAMED Firestore database, never (default). In a shared project (default)
# belongs to whoever created it first, and purge-data.sh refuses to operate on
# it for exactly that reason.
FIRESTORE_DATABASE=swarm

# ARTIFACT_BUCKET=saga-agents-staging-swarm-artifacts
# ARTIFACT_REGISTRY=swarm-images

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Terraform
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The swarm's own state bucket, created by terraform/bootstrap. NOT
# saga-agents-terraform-state-staging -- that bucket belongs to another team and
# is on the deny-list.
# TF_STATE_BUCKET=swarm-tfstate-saga-agents-staging
# TF_STATE_PREFIX=infra/dev

# Pinned tool paths. Set these when the version that wins $PATH is the wrong
# one; see docs/versions.md for why this is not hypothetical on macOS.
# SWARM_TERRAFORM=$HOME/.local/bin/terraform
# SWARM_TFLINT=$HOME/.local/bin/tflint
# SWARM_CHECKOV=$HOME/.local/bin/checkov
# SWARM_TRIVY=$HOME/.local/bin/trivy
# SWARM_KUBECTL=/opt/homebrew/bin/kubectl

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compute
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The swarm's OWN Autopilot cluster, used only for browser/GPU/>32GiB work.
# `agents-staging` is another team's cluster and configure-kubectl.sh refuses to
# fetch credentials for it.
# GKE_CLUSTER=swarm-autopilot
# GKE_LOCATION=us-central1

# Cloud Run service names (terraform/infra/locals.tf owns these).
# API_SERVICE=swarm-api
# SCHEDULER_SERVICE=swarm-scheduler
# QUOTA_SERVICE=swarm-quota-broker
# RECONCILER_SERVICE=swarm-reconciler

# PUBSUB_TOPIC=swarm-scheduler-wake
# SCHEDULER_JOB=swarm-scheduler-tick

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API access
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Resolved from terraform output, then from `gcloud run services describe`.
# Set it only to talk to something else (a local `make dev`, say).
# API_URL=https://swarm-api-xxxxxxxxxx-uc.a.run.app
# API_PREFIX=/v1
# HTTP_TIMEOUT=30

# The audience the API accepts in an ID token. Leave empty to accept the
# operator's own gcloud token; set it when minting tokens by impersonation.
# API_AUDIENCE=

# CI sets SWARM_ID_TOKEN from Workload Identity Federation. Locally, prefer
# impersonation over pasting a token anywhere.
# SWARM_IMPERSONATE_SA=swarm-ci@saga-agents-staging.iam.gserviceaccount.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tenancy (used by register-tenant.sh; the API reads its own copy from Terraform)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Admin-registered tenant groups in PRIORITY ORDER -- first match wins, which is
# what makes a user who is in several groups land in the same tenant on every
# request. Enumerating a caller's groups is not possible in this project (see
# CONTRACT.md), so the API checks membership group by group against this list.
# TENANT_GROUPS=eng@saga.xyz,research@saga.xyz

# Membership in any of these grants the admin surface (/v1/admin/*).
# ADMIN_GROUPS=swarm-admins@saga.xyz

# Only these hosted domains may authenticate. There is no shared bearer token.
# ALLOWED_DOMAINS=saga.xyz

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local development (`make dev`, docker-compose.yml)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_PORT=8080
# DEV_APP_PORT=8000

# There is deliberately NO switch here that turns authentication off. The API
# refuses REQUIRE_AUTH=false outright rather than ignoring it, because a setting
# that reads like "auth is off" and silently does nothing is worse than no
# setting at all. The local loop authenticates with a real Google ID token from
# `gcloud auth print-identity-token`, exactly as a deployed caller does.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Behaviour toggles the scripts respect
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Skips confirmations for NON-destructive flows only. destroy.sh and
# purge-data.sh ignore it on purpose and always require a typed confirmation.
# SWARM_ASSUME_YES=1

# NO_COLOR=1
